---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
import { ISSUES_URL, REPO_LINKS_ENABLED, APP_VERSION } from '../site';

const title = 'Support: troubleshooting Krova and reporting a problem';
const description =
  'Fixes for the real issues you can hit with Krova: nothing happens on Shift-drag, a keyboard shortcut does nothing, the tray icon or window is missing, skipped windows, windows not returning after a monitor change, linked dividers, SmartScreen, macOS permissions, resetting the config. And how to report a problem.';

const toc = [
  ['nothing-happens', 'Nothing happens when I Shift-drag'],
  ['shortcut', 'A keyboard shortcut does nothing'],
  ['tray', 'I cannot find the tray icon or the window'],
  ['skipped', 'One window is never snapped'],
  ['size', 'The window is not exactly the zone size'],
  ['blurry', 'Krova’s window looks blurry'],
  ['smartscreen', 'Windows says “Windows protected your PC”'],
  ['macos', 'macOS: Accessibility or Gatekeeper'],
  ['reconnect', 'Layouts did not come back after reconnecting a monitor'],
  ['windows-back', 'Windows did not return to their zones'],
  ['linked', 'Linked dividers do nothing'],
  ['reset', 'Reset Krova to defaults'],
  ['report', 'Report a problem'],
] as const;
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Support</p>
      <h1>Troubleshooting</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The problems you are most likely to hit, with what to check. There is no support e-mail; if this page does not
        solve it, <a href="#report">report a problem</a>.
      </p>
    </header>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <ul>
        {toc.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <article class="prose">
      <h2 id="nothing-happens">Nothing happens when I Shift-drag</h2>
      <p>Work down this list:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Is Krova running? Its icon is in the tray or menu bar (see below if you cannot see it).</li>
        <li>Is <strong>Enabled</strong> ticked in the tray menu?</li>
        <li>
          Are you dragging a normal window by its title bar, and holding the key from Settings, Activation modifier?
          It is Shift unless you changed it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Is the window one Krova skips: a minimized or fullscreen window, or (on Windows) an app running as
          administrator? See <a href="#skipped">below</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Are you using Krova&rsquo;s edge snapping expecting zones, or the other way round? Edge snapping needs no key and
          only works at the screen edges; zones need the key and show anywhere.
        </li>
        <li>On macOS: has Krova been given Accessibility access? See <a href="#macos">below</a>.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="shortcut">A keyboard shortcut does nothing</h2>
      <p>
        The shortcuts (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd> and Left, Right, Up or 1 to 9 by default) exist on Windows
        only; macOS has none yet. On Windows, work down this list:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Is <strong>Enabled</strong> ticked in the tray menu? When it is off, the shortcuts are released.</li>
        <li>Open <strong>Settings</strong> and look at <strong>Keyboard shortcuts</strong>: is <em>Use keyboard shortcuts</em> on?</li>
        <li>
          Does the same card list shortcuts that &ldquo;could not be registered&rdquo;? Then Windows or another app already owns that
          combination. Some combinations are kept by Windows: on the PC we tested it refused every Win+Alt and eleven of twelve Win+Ctrl
          combinations. Choose other <em>Modifier keys</em> (the list updates at once), or close the app that holds the keys. Some
          tools also use Ctrl+Alt+Left and Right for back and forward.
        </li>
        <li>
          Is the focused window one the shortcuts skip? They do nothing for the desktop, the taskbar, minimized windows, windows of
          programs running as administrator, and Krova&rsquo;s own windows.
        </li>
        <li>
          Is the zone there? <kbd>1</kbd> to <kbd>9</kbd> count zones top to bottom, then left to right, on the monitor the window is
          on; a number past the last zone does nothing, and <kbd>Up</kbd> does nothing with a single monitor.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        See <a href={url('/guides/keyboard-shortcuts/')}>Keyboard shortcuts</a> for the full map.
      </p>

      <h2 id="tray">I cannot find the tray icon or the window</h2>
      <p>
        Krova starts with no window, so its icon is the only sign it is running. On Windows, new icons are often hidden
        behind the <strong>^</strong> arrow at the right end of the taskbar: click it, and drag the Krova icon out to keep it
        visible. On macOS, look in the menu bar. If it is not there at all, start Krova from the Start menu or Applications;
        a second launch opens the first one&rsquo;s editor window instead of starting another copy.
      </p>
      <p>
        Closing Krova&rsquo;s window closes it for good, together with the embedded browser, and Krova keeps running in the tray.
        That is expected: it keeps a tray-only Krova small. To get the window back, choose <strong>Edit layouts&hellip;</strong> or
        <strong>Settings&hellip;</strong> in the tray menu, or start Krova again. Unsaved layout edits are lost when the window
        closes. To stop Krova, choose <strong>Quit Krova</strong> in the tray menu.
      </p>

      <h2 id="skipped">One window is never snapped</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Apps running as administrator (Windows).</strong> Windows does not let a normal program move the windows
          of an elevated one, so Krova skips them. Running Krova itself as administrator is not supported.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Fullscreen and borderless fullscreen windows</strong> are skipped.</li>
        <li><strong>Minimized windows</strong> are skipped.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>You pressed on a resize edge</strong>, or the window never moved: that is not a drag, so nothing
          happens.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="size">The window is not exactly the zone size</h2>
      <p>
        Some apps enforce their own minimum or maximum size. Krova respects it, so the window may come out larger or smaller
        than the zone, or sit inside it. This is the app refusing, not a zone that is wrong.
      </p>

      <h2 id="blurry">Krova&rsquo;s window looks blurry</h2>
      <p>
        Krova now declares per-monitor DPI awareness, which is meant to keep its own window (the editor and settings) sharp on
        displays scaled above 100%. We have not verified that on a mixed-scale setup, for example a 100% monitor next to a 150% one.
        If the window still looks blurry, or zones land in the wrong place on a scaled monitor, it is worth reporting, with your
        scaling settings. It is not a setting you got wrong.
      </p>

      <h2 id="smartscreen">Windows says &ldquo;Windows protected your PC&rdquo;</h2>
      <p>
        The installer is not code-signed, so SmartScreen does not recognise it. Select <strong>More info</strong>, then
        <strong>Run anyway</strong>. See <a href={url('/guides/install/#windows')}>the install guide</a>, and
        <a href={url('/download/#verify')}>how to check the file</a> first if you want to.
      </p>

      <h2 id="macos">macOS: Accessibility or Gatekeeper</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Snapping does nothing.</strong> Krova needs Accessibility access: System Settings, Privacy &amp; Security,
          Accessibility, then turn on Krova. If it is already on, switch it off and on again. Krova shows a banner until
          access is granted. Until it is, Krova cannot watch the mouse at all; after you grant it, you may need to quit and
          start Krova again.
        </li>
        <li><strong>It may also ask for Input Monitoring.</strong> Allow it under Privacy &amp; Security if it does.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>&ldquo;Krova cannot be opened.&rdquo;</strong> The app is unsigned and not notarised. Right-click it in
          Applications and choose Open, or use <strong>Open Anyway</strong> in Privacy &amp; Security.
        </li>
        <li><strong>The macOS build has never been tested on a real Mac.</strong> Anything else going wrong is worth reporting.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="reconnect">Layouts did not come back after reconnecting a monitor</h2>
      <p>
        Krova matches monitors by hardware identity, then device path, then last size and position. Monitors that report
        little identity, or two identical models, can be confused. See
        <a href={url('/guides/multiple-displays/#identical')}>Multiple displays</a>. Open the editor, check each monitor&rsquo;s
        tab, and set the active layout.
      </p>

      <h2 id="windows-back">Windows did not return to their zones</h2>
      <p>
        This is a Windows-only feature (<strong>Restore windows on reconnect</strong>, Settings, Windows). It is early: it has been
        tested with a simulated monitor change, not yet with a real unplug and replug. Things to check:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>The setting is on, and <strong>Enabled</strong> is ticked in the tray menu.</li>
        <li>
          The window was snapped by Krova (by dragging into a zone, or by a shortcut) since Krova last started. Krova remembers nothing
          after you quit it, and it forgets a window as soon as you drag, move or maximize it yourself.
        </li>
        <li>
          The monitor it was on is the one that came back, and Krova recognised it as the same monitor (see the
          <a href="#reconnect">section above</a>). Give it a couple of seconds after the display settles: Krova waits about 1.5 seconds
          for the change to stop before it moves anything.
        </li>
        <li>
          Windows 11 has its own setting that restores window positions when you re-dock. Krova leaves a window that is already in its
          zone, so you may simply see Windows&rsquo; own restore.
        </li>
        <li>An app with a minimum size may sit larger than its zone; that is the app refusing, not a miss.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="linked">Linked dividers do nothing</h2>
      <p>
        Linked dividers (<strong>Linked dividers</strong>, Settings, Windows) are early and Windows only. After you snap windows side
        by side, drag the edge of one of them and let go of the mouse: the snapped windows that share that edge follow. They move when
        you release, not while you drag. The part that moves the neighbours has been tested with synthetic events; we have not yet
        confirmed that Windows reports a real mouse drag to Krova, so it may not work on your PC at all, and we would like to hear
        either way. It only acts on windows Krova snapped on the same monitor and layout that are still in their zones. If you do not
        want the behaviour, switch the setting off.
      </p>

      <h2 id="reset">Reset Krova to defaults</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Choose <strong>Quit Krova</strong> from the tray menu.</li>
        <li>
          Delete <code>config.json</code> in <code>%APPDATA%\dev.krova.desktop</code> (Windows) or
          <code>~/Library/Application Support/dev.krova.desktop</code> (macOS).
        </li>
        <li>Start Krova. It creates a fresh file with default settings and the four template layouts per monitor.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        You do not always have to do this. If Krova finds a <code>config.json</code> it cannot read, it renames it to
        <code>config.json.bak</code> and starts with defaults. If the file was written by a <em>newer</em> Krova, this
        version leaves it alone and runs on defaults without saving, and does not tell you that your changes are not being
        kept.
      </p>

      <h2 id="report">Report a problem</h2>
      {
        REPO_LINKS_ENABLED ? (
          <p>
            Open an issue on <a href={ISSUES_URL}>GitHub</a>. Search the existing ones first. There is no e-mail address
            for support.
          </p>
        ) : (
          <div class="note note--warn">
            <p>
              <strong>Krova&rsquo;s repository is not public yet,</strong> so there is nowhere to file an issue yet. This
              page will link to it once it is.
            </p>
          </div>
        )
      }
      <p>A report is easiest to act on when it says:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The Krova version (the footer of Krova&rsquo;s window reads &ldquo;Krova v{APP_VERSION}&rdquo;) and where you got it.</li>
        <li>Your operating system and its exact version, Windows 10 or 11 or the macOS version.</li>
        <li>Your monitors: how many, their resolutions, and their scaling (for example 150%).</li>
        <li>Which app's window you were dragging, and whether it runs as administrator.</li>
        <li>What you did, what you expected, and what happened, in order.</li>
        <li>Your activation key, whether edge snapping is on, and for a shortcut problem the modifier keys you chose and any shortcuts Settings lists as not registered.</li>
        <li>For a crash or a missing tray icon: whether you installed with the installer, and whether Krova was running before.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="note">
        <p>
          <strong>Before you share your config.</strong> <code>config.json</code> can help, but it contains your monitors&rsquo;
          identifiers: the hardware id, the device path (which includes an identifier for the port) and each monitor&rsquo;s
          size and position. Remove or blank those fields first if you would rather not share them. Window titles are never
          stored, so there is nothing about your windows in it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .toc {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
    padding: var(--space-m);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-l);
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }
  .toc ul {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.1rem 2rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
  }
  .toc a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 2.25rem;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
  article {
    padding-bottom: var(--section-y);
  }
  article :global(h2:first-child) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
